@prefix pm:      <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology#> .
@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix pmp:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/practices/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .

#
# prima-materia — two protocols opened against two generalizations by one
# reading, and both return a deviation
#
# Neither of these was the question the run set out with. The Rosicrucian
# corpus was taken off the register on the structure-driven entrance of SPEC
# 14, which is the entrance that produces claims nobody asked for; what it
# produced here is a counter-case to a statement that stood on six witnesses
# and a counter-case to a statement that stood on two, from the same two days
# of the same work.
#
# Both statements name their own falsifying condition and both conditions are
# met by wording in the operative passage. Neither statement is rewritten: a
# claim is corrected where it is examined, and what a later reader needs is the
# protocol beside it and not a tidied sentence. The nodes the protocols rest on
# are in traditions/rosicrucian.ttl.
#
# One thing the two protocols share and it should be said once rather than
# twice. The work is a narrated vision, not a report of a practice, so what it
# witnesses is what the transmission holds and not what happened. That is
# enough for both statements, because both are about what transmissions state.
#

pmp:TokenGateFailureExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Whether a gate that hands over its own token ever states a failure at it"@en, "Ob ein Tor, das seinen eigenen Ausweis übergibt, je ein Misslingen an ihm nennt"@de ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the first limb of the claim that where a transmission carries a graded passage in which the passer is admitted at each station by producing a token that the station itself or the transmission supplies him, it states no test at any station and names no case in which a passer failed to be admitted. The question put was the one the claim names as its own falsifier: whether the holding carries a token-handing passage that nevertheless states a test, names a refusal, or says what a wrong token would cost."@en ;
    pm:tests pmc:ThePassageThatPutsNoQuestion ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A transmission in which a station admits the passer on a token the station or the text supplies, and which nevertheless states a test there, or names a case in which somebody was refused, or states what a wrong token would cost. That is the condition the claim itself named. It is met."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:casesWithDeviation ;
    pm:counterSearch pm:counterSearchFoundNothing ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "The counter-search here is a search for a reading under which the case is not a case, and it was carried before the verdict was set. Three ways out were looked for and none holds. First, that the gates of the Chymische Hochzeit are not token-handing: they are, and more strictly than the four witnesses of the first limb, because the token of each station is bought from that station for a consumable the traveller happens to be carrying — the bottle of water at the first, the salt at the second — and the letter to the second porter is written by the first. Second, that the refusal is not at the station: it is, the gate being the thing that shuts. Third, that the refusal stands in a frame and not in the operative passage: it does not, it stands in the same paragraph as the issuing of the second token and is reported of persons, those who stood ready without and called at the gate. Searched over the compiled holding besides for any further token-handing passage carrying a stated failure, over the five ascent traditions the claim's own counter-search went through; none was found, so this protocol adds one case and not a class."@en ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-30" ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-30" ;
    skos:note "The case, in the terms the claim uses. The second day of the Chymische Hochzeit carries a graded passage of three gates. At the first the porter demands the letter of invitation — which the bridegroom sent the traveller, so the transmission supplied it — is satisfied by it, sells him a golden token marked S.C. for his bottle of water, and gives him besides a sealed letter to the second porter. At the second the porter reads that letter, drives back a chained lion, and sells a token marked S.M. for his salt. At the third his name is written in a vellum book, the true guest token S.P.N. is given him, smaller and much heavier than the others, and new shoes are given while the old are to be handed to the poor. Nothing the traveller brought of himself opens any gate. The arrangement is the one the claim describes: the token of the next station is issued at the last."@en ;
    skos:note "And the same passage states two failures at those stations. The gate shuts: as the traveller lingers with the second porter the castle bell rings, the porter tells him to run or all the pains and labour he has hitherto undergone will serve to no purpose, the lights above begin to go out, he gets in beside the virgin who extinguishes them, the gate is clapped to, part of his coat is locked out and left behind, and neither he nor they who stood ready without and called at the gate could prevail with the porter to open it again. The porter then hands the keys to the virgin. Second, and stated as a class rather than an event: the first porter tells him how easily he might have forgotten the letter of invitation, as it also chanced to others. That is a set of applicants who came to a token-handing station and lacked the token the transmission had issued them."@en ;
    skos:note "What the deviation costs the claim, put as narrowly as it can be. The first limb is falsified as written: there exists a token-handing passage that names a refusal at a station. What survives is the observation the limb was built on, which is that the four ascent witnesses state none — and the correction the case suggests is not that the limb be widened but that it be split. In the Jeu and Pistis Sophia material the token is read off the passer by the watchers, who do not speak with him; here it is sold to him at a counter and the transaction takes time. A station that transacts can be reached too late, and a station that reads cannot. That is a property of the gate and not of the tradition, and it is the shape a rewritten statement should have."@en ;
    skos:note "The second feature of the case is one the claim did not ask about and it belongs in the record. The refusal is put beyond the officiant: the porter cannot reopen the gate, and he divests himself of the keys in the same sentence. So the transmission states a failure at its own station and simultaneously removes the person at the station from the power to remit it. Both halves are in the operative passage, and this holding already carries the second half as a form of its own at pmc:TheExcuseThatStopsAtTheOfficiant, where it works the other way round."@en ;
    skos:note "What this protocol does not settle, and it is the part a later reader should not take from it. Whether the Chymische Hochzeit's gates have a documented route to the Hekhalot and kabbalistic material this holding carries was not established and no search for one was carried; the corpus is late, Western and syncretistic, and the Fama names its own antecedents freely. The falsification does not need independence — one clean case of the named kind ends the limb whatever its lineage — but a rewritten statement that wanted to count transmissions rather than cases would need it, and it is not here."@en .

pmp:GradedTrialExclusionExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Whether a set of trials ever states plain exclusion for partial failure"@en, "Ob ein Satz von Proben je den glatten Ausschluss für teilweises Versagen nennt"@de ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the claim that where a transmission makes a set of trials or obstacles the condition of its highest attainment, it also states an outcome for the candidate who clears only part of the set, and that outcome is a lesser rank within the same system rather than exclusion from it; and that the sets are so constructed that a candidate cannot establish in advance which trials he faces or how many he has cleared, so the grading is assigned after the fact. The question put was the falsifier the claim names: a set of trials that states a plain exclusion for partial failure."@en ;
    pm:tests pmc:TheTrialWithNoFailingGrade ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A transmission with a set of trials that states a plain exclusion for partial failure — the candidate obtains nothing, is not of the company, is sent away. That is the condition the claim itself named. It is met, and the exclusion is itself graded."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:casesWithDeviation ;
    pm:counterSearch pm:counterSearchFoundNothing ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "The counter-search looked for a reading under which the weighing is not a set of trials, which is the escape the claim used against the Mithraic material: that case was set aside as a single ordeal and the claim is about sets. Here the escape is unavailable. There are seven weights, they are put in one after another, they are numbered, the sentence of the same day grades its punishments by which of them a guest failed, and the work publishes a distribution over the number cleared. A second reading was looked for as well — that the guests who fail are not candidates for the highest attainment but gatecrashers, and so outside the antecedent. It does not hold either: they came on the bridegroom's invitation, were admitted at the gates on his tokens, and the sentence dismisses some of them as lords and potentates expressly rejected by this our Order, which is a rejection of candidates and not the removal of intruders. Searched besides over the compiled holding for any further set of trials stating plain exclusion, in the Mithraic, Midewiwin, Ars notoria and solomonic material the claim names as the places to widen it; none of the four was found to state one, so this protocol adds a case and not a class."@en ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-30" ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-30" ;
    skos:note "The case. On the third day of the Chymische Hochzeit a balance of gold is hung in the hall and seven weights are set out; two hundred armed men are divided into seven bands by the number of the weights; each guest is weighed against them one by one, and he who cannot raise a weight is flung out of the scale, bound, laughed at and delivered to the band of the weight he failed. The sentence read the same day assigns the outcome by which weights were failed: those who did not stand up to the first, third and fourth are stripped stark naked and sent forth; those too light in the second and fifth are besides stripping branded with one, two or more marks according as each was lighter or heavier; those drawn up by the sixth or seventh and not by the rest are dealt with somewhat more graciously; for every combination a certain punishment was ordained; and the cheaters who could move no weight at all are punished in body and life with the sword, halter, water and rods."@en ;
    skos:note "That is the claim's second clause exactly inverted. The grading is there and it is finer than in either of the claim's two witnesses, which have twelve trials and nine difficulties and one lesser rank each; here seven weights generate a punishment for every combination. But every grade is a way of being put out. Nobody who failed a weight receives a lesser rank inside the system, and the dismissal says so in its own words: you cannot be present at his Majesty's feast of honour, neither is your reputation hereby prejudiced although you be rejected by this our Order. The transmission grades the exclusion and not the attainment."@en ;
    skos:note "The third clause fails too and more cheaply. The claim says the sets are so constructed that a candidate cannot establish in advance which trials he faces or how many he has cleared. Here he is told the evening before that the artist's scales will be set on the morrow's morn, wherein each one shall be weighed and found what he has forgotten; the weighing is public, in the middle of the hall, before the whole company; and each man's result is recorded, the narrator saying he numbered them as they stood before him and noted them in his table-book. What he cannot establish in advance is what the weights measure, which is a different defect and the one pmp:RosicrucianDignitasSearch exhibits."@en ;
    skos:note "What the deviation costs and what it leaves. The claim's antecedent is a set of trials conditioning a highest attainment; its consequent has three parts, and this case breaks the second and the third while confirming the first. So the surviving statement is thin: transmissions with sets of trials state an outcome for partial clearing. Whether that outcome is a lesser rank or an exclusion now has both values in the holding, and the interesting question is what predicts which — the two witnesses of the lesser rank are Chinese cultivation lineages in which the candidate is already inside the transmission, and the case here is an admission trial at the threshold of one. A candidate who is already in cannot be excluded and a candidate at the door cannot be demoted, which would make the claim's consequent a function of where the trial sits. That is a statement worth forming and it is not formed here, because two witnesses on one side and one on the other is not enough and because a claim is not to be replaced in the protocol that broke it."@en ;
    skos:note "One feature of the case bears on the claim's own reason for existing and cuts the other way. The claim's second note says that a graded trial produces a set of people who each attained something, so that the transmission's own records contain no failures at all. This transmission's record contains nothing but failures, counted: seven who raised one weight, twenty-one who raised two, thirty-five three, thirty-five four, twenty-one five, seven six, one who reached the seventh and could not raise it, many who wholly failed, and few who raised them all. The numbers are the binomial coefficients and are therefore constructed rather than counted, which is entered at pmc:RosicrucianDistributioPonderum and does not affect this protocol: what matters here is that the transmission publishes a failure rate at all, and no other transmission in this holding does."@en .
